In no particular order, approximately fifteen/twenty years ago, a rupture that was already occuring in machine learning fractured. We now have applied machine learning and academic (basic) machine learning, and the divide between these two could not be greater.

Applied machine learning is rife with paper mills and poor study design, and methodology that does not warrant the term, to say the least. It is loaded with competing interests and conflicts of interest driven by business and ideeology. On the other hand, academic or basic machine learning is not in practice, beyond niche lab groups and is largely unused, which makes it useless to a large degree, even though it is not inherently so. While these are sweeping over simplifications they are not untrue.

